{
  "abstract": "We study biplanar graphs through their attachments to a specified eight-clique. We prove that every such graph is nine-colorable if each outside vertex has at least four neighbors in the clique. A fixed clique coloring gives variable lists containing a common ninth name. Hereditary Euler bounds and list-criticality leave at most sixteen outside vertices. The accepted twenty-vertex theorem handles the smaller instances; Gallai-forest identities and explicit surplus-color choices eliminate the remaining orders, giving a complete hand proof. With only three anchor neighbors, we show that any non-nine-colorable graph contains an induced obstruction on at most $189$ vertices, with at most sixteen vertices exceeding their available-list degrees. The high set cannot have size one or consist of two independent vertices. An adjacent pair must force equal colors across a sharply restricted low forest; an exact forty-six-pattern relation-cover certificate excludes clique and end-six-clique components alone. This is a structural reduction, not a complete exclusion. Under the stronger six-neighbor condition, we prove that all named nine-colorings are Kempe equivalent and that any one outside terminal can avoid the name absent from the clique. This last result needs only $K_9$-freeness and a global Euler edge bound. Two independently written finite implementations verify the complete unmarked and marked list cores, using first-appearance colors and occurrence-support multisets. We credit the classical degree-choosability and list-Kempe deletion mechanisms and distinguish the imported small-order theorem from the new evidence. These results constrain possible counterexamples but do not establish occurrence of an eight-clique or resolve unrestricted Earth–Moon.",
